<Layout title="Tarian Compute — Exploring AI infrastructure in South Wales" description="A Welsh company exploring compute opportunities in South Wales: the AI Growth Zone, power, connectivity, sites and demand. Research and early conversations.">
  <PageIntro label="Tarian Compute / A Welsh perspective" title="Understanding what makes compute viable."><p>Tarian Compute is an early-stage initiative from Tarian Ventures, a Welsh company based in Cardiff. We are exploring how commercially viable compute infrastructure could develop in South Wales.</p><p>The South Wales AI Growth Zone provides a timely context for that work. Our focus is on the practical conditions behind the opportunity: demand, power, connectivity and sites.</p><p class="status">Research and early conversations</p></PageIntro>
  <section class="section shell split ruled-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Why South Wales</p><h2>A regional opportunity.<br />A Welsh perspective.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p class="lead">The South Wales AI Growth Zone places the region within the UK’s plans to develop AI infrastructure.</p><p>Announced in November 2025, the zone includes sites along the M4 corridor from Newport to Bridgend. For Tarian, this creates a reason to investigate where a Welsh business could contribute and what would make a proposition viable.</p><p>Our work is independent and at the research stage. Tarian is not an appointed Growth Zone delivery partner and does not claim government backing.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://businesswales.gov.wales/news-and-blog/second-ai-growth-zone-wales-announced">Read the announcement on Business Wales <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
  <section class="section shell split ruled-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">The questions shaping our work</p><h2>Start with the conditions.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p class="lead">Compute infrastructure depends on more than the technology inside a facility.</p><p>We are investigating how power availability, network connectivity, development requirements and future demand come together—and where the practical constraints sit.</p><p>Compute is the current priority within Tarian’s wider work exploring and developing new ventures.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section tint-section"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Areas of investigation</p><div class="research-grid">
    <section><span class="research-number">01</span><h2>Power &amp; location</h2><p>Understanding grid capacity and connection timelines, potential sites and planning requirements in South Wales.</p></section>
    <section><span class="research-number">02</span><h2>Connectivity &amp; demand</h2><p>Examining fibre connectivity, latency and the needs of organisations that could use compute capacity in the region and beyond.</p></section>
    <section><span class="research-number">03</span><h2>Delivery &amp; viability</h2><p>Testing build and operating costs, delivery requirements and where Welsh expertise and suppliers could contribute.</p></section>
  </div></div></section>
  <section class="section shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">A useful first conversation</p><h2>Bring a perspective.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p class="lead">We would like to hear from people working in energy, land and site development, fibre networks, data centre delivery or compute demand, particularly those with practical knowledge of South Wales.</p><p>You may have a practical constraint to share, an opportunity worth examining or expertise that could help test our assumptions.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/#compute">Discuss Tarian Compute <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
</Layout>
